  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm temperatures today and Friday \ncould approach daily record highs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA stationary front remains parked over northern Maine early this\nmorning, and should slowly begin to lift northwards as a warm\nfront through the mid morning hours today. With the parent\nsurface low remaining over the Great Lakes at this time, this\nputs Maine well within the warm sector of the low, with steady\nsouthwest warm air advection maintaining above average\ntemperatures through the day today, even as cloud cover may\nlinger over the northern half of the state.\n\nElevated low level moisture persists behind the recent rainfall,\nleading to patchy to areas of dense fog early this morning. This\nfog could return tonight, especially in areas which hold onto\nhigh dewpoints into this evening. A brief period of light rain\nearly this evening will sweep through the CWA, and areas which\nreceive additional rainfall will be most susceptible to the\nreturn of dense fog overnight.\n\nFriday will feature another round of anomalously warm\ntemperatures at least 10 degrees above average, however\nincreasing cloud cover ahead of the next low pressure system and\ndecreased warm air advection may lead to temperatures not quite\nas warm as earlier in the week, limiting but not completely\neliminating the chance for reaching daily record high\ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional rainfall expected Friday into the\nweekend, though amounts are still uncertain. The rainfall could\nhelp provide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper-level low pressure system from the Great Lakes region\nmoves east on Friday. This system helps develop a coastal low \nthat will move NE along the New England coast. Models continue \nto struggle with the exact location of the low track, which is \ngoing to impact how far north precipitation reaches. Generally, \nmodels are in agreement that the storm system with move along \nthe Gulf of Maine. As a result, the Downeast region has the best\nchance for higher rain totals. After this, uncertainty then \ndevelops Saturday into Sunday regarding the track of the surface\nlow. Global models are struggling with how the coastal low will\ninteract with the upper-low. A track closer to the Downeast \ncoast would bring better rain chances to the entire forecast \narea. A track across the Gulf of Maine to Nova Scotia would keep\nbetter rain chances across Downeast areas, with less rain \ntotals northward across the remainder of the forecast area. \nPrecipitation totals Friday through Sunday will be dependent on \nthe eventual track of the low. As of this update, chance for \n1-1.5 inches for Downeast coast, 0.5-1.0 inches for interior \nDowneast, 0.1-0.5 inches in the north, Friday through Sunday.",
